arrowExprT -- SEQ 595 R2 (Tony, 2026-11-10): PIN, DO NOT CURE. A <- with nothing on its left, inside an ordinary
expression. The old road refuses it by name ("an operator in a prefix position with no target", SEQ 347 R4) and the
statement stores or prints nothing. The new road drops the <- silently and uses the operand: this is why FORBACk must
sit before FORWARd (a forward for would take `<- source` as its source and run forwards). Intended on the new road:
refused by name, the old road's value. The cure is its own later stroke. Rows prefixed AX-.

Not run alone: pop.sh runs each row twice, one process each: on the old road (the statements at top level) and on the
new road (the same statements through FILe), then print "Q" and a sentinel. Each reads exit and sentinel, stdout
(lines joined by |), and the count of REFUSED lines naming <-.

AX-1  an assignment's right side.        AX-2  a print item.        AX-3  a condition (neither arm on the old road).
AX-4  after another operand, still with no target of its own.
AX-5  control: an ordinary rebind, x <- y, is not refused on either road.

ROWS, separated by ^ -- id ^ statements ^ old road stdout ^ old road REFUSED <- lines ^ new road stdout today ^ new road REFUSED <- lines today
AX-1^qr = <- qb; print qr:;^0 |Q |^1^3 |Q |^0
AX-2^print <- qb:;^Q |^1^3 |Q |^0
AX-3^if <- qa; print 1:; else print 2:;^Q |^1^1 |Q |^0
AX-4^qr = qa + <- qb; print qr:;^0 |Q |^1^5 |Q |^0
AX-5^qr <- qb; print *qr:;^3 |Q |^0^3 |Q |^0
